StFttDbMaker/macro -- FTT (sTGC) geometry (survey/alignment) tables
===================================================================

What is here
------------
  ftt/*.C                 the Geometry/ftt survey tables, one file per
                          (table, beginTime) pair, in St_db_Maker's
                          <table>.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.C naming
  fttGeometry_db.C        reads those files and pushes them to the offline
                          MySQL DB (see "Pushing to the database" below)

Three tables are chained by StFttDb::loadGeometry():

      stgcOnTpc           whole sTGC inside the TPC frame             (1 row)
      stationOnStgc       each of the 4 stations on the sTGC          (4 rows)
      pentOnStation       each of the 16 pentagons on its station     (16 rows)

  row = 4*station + k, with k: 0=A 1=D 2=C 3=B. AGML orders the pentagons by
  rotation (A,D,C,B) while StFttDb numbers quadrants A,B,C,D, so the two
  conventions differ and the map was measured from the built geometry, not
  assumed. See script/pentMap.C.

NAMESPACE: the DB namespace is Geometry/ftt. It used to be Geometry/stgc; the
rename is PR #20 (the StgmGeo1.xml <Misalign> paths) plus PR #23 (the
StFttDb::loadGeometry() half). Table NAMES did not change -- only the
namespace -- which is why stgcOnTpc and stationOnStgc still read "stgc".
Before those PRs land, the local-override directory is ./StarDb/Geometry/stgc;
after, it is ./StarDb/Geometry/ftt. Use the one matching the code you run.


Using these tables locally instead of the database
--------------------------------------------------
St_db_Maker does NOT give ./StarDb priority. This is the most misunderstood
thing here, so it is measured and quoted rather than asserted:

  * All .C files found in ANY search directory are pooled, and
    St_db_Maker::FindLeft() takes the one whose timestamp is LATEST at or
    before the requested time. Which directory it came from is irrelevant.
  * When both a file and a MySQL row exist, St_db_Maker.cxx:827 decides:
        case 3:   // Both SQL and CINT objects
          if (valsCINT[0].Get()>=valsSQL[0].Get()) { kase = 2; ... }  // file
          else                                     { kase = 1; ... }  // MySQL
    again the LATER beginTime wins. Files do not categorically beat the DB.
  * The list StBFChain builds is MySQL, $STAR/StarDb,
    $STAR/.$STAR_HOST_SYS/obj/StarDb, $PWD/StarDb, $PWD/obj/StarDb --
    $PWD/StarDb is FOURTH. Position in that list is not precedence.

So "put it in ./StarDb and it wins" is false. What wins is the latest
beginTime. To override locally, your file needs a timestamp later than every
other entry for that table, the release's included.

  MEASURED 2026-10-13 on FST, requesting a Run 22 date with ./StarDb populated:
      local files named 2021111x  ->  ours won     fstOnTpc=(+0.14876,-0.31702)
      local files named 2021101x  ->  RELEASE won  hssOnFst=(+0.07126,-0.30102)
  The second is the state after the rename into Gene's slots: the release ships
  hssOnFst at 20211110.000001, LATER than our 20211015.000001, so it wins and
  edits to our local copies are silently ignored. The resulting geometry is
  unaffected -- the two decompositions sum to the same translation -- but an
  edit does nothing, with no warning.

  Until the release copy is gone, override with a later timestamp: copy the
  file you want to <table>.20211113.000000.C in ./StarDb and edit that.

To put files there at all:


      mkdir -p ./StarDb/Geometry
      cp -r $STAR/StRoot/StFttDbMaker/macro/ftt ./StarDb/Geometry/ftt
      # or: ln -s $STAR/StRoot/StFttDbMaker/macro/ftt ./StarDb/Geometry/ftt
      # (./StarDb/Geometry/stgc if you are on code from before the rename)

  * REMEMBER TO REMOVE IT when you want the database values back.

  * CONDOR JOBS run in their own scratch directory, so ./StarDb must exist
    THERE, not where you submitted from. Copy or link the ftt dir into the
    job's working/tmp directory too, or the job quietly uses the DB and your
    test shows no change.

  * THE GEOMETRY CACHE MUST MATCH. StFwdTrackMaker reads the cached
    fGeom.*.root, not these tables. Rebuild it after any change:

        root4star -b -q 'build_geom_misalign.C("dev2022sm","20211112.000003",1)'
            -> fGeom.dev2022sm.misalign1.sdt20211112.000003.root

    The sdt argument picks which table version AgPosition resolves to (latest
    entry at or before it) and misalign=1 is what makes it read the survey
    tables at all. Ship the fGeom file with the job as well.

  * PER-QUADRANT z NEEDS THE FLOAT-ZPLANE GEOMETRY. From iteration 2 onward
    pentOnStation carries a non-zero t2. That is measured against a geometry
    built with zplane as float (PR #20); against the old int-truncated zplane
    the z terms are referenced to the wrong plane positions.


What each tag means
-------------------
Translations are cm. No rotation has ever been non-identity in these tables.
stationOnStgc is identity in every version to date.

  tag          files (per table)                beginTime              meaning
  -----------  -------------------------------  ---------------------  ---------
  run22ideal   all three .20211110.000001       2021-10-10 00:00:00    initial: no shift,
                                                                        no rotation
  run22iter1   onTpc/stat .20211112.000001      2021-11-12 00:00:00    iteration 1 by Akio:
               pent       .20211112.000001                              per-quadrant x/y from
                                                                        FST->sTGC residuals
  run22iter2   onTpc/stat .20211112.000001      2021-11-12 00:00:02    iteration 2: adds the
               pent       .20211112.000002                              PER-QUADRANT z (t2);
                                                                        x/y unchanged
  run22iter3   onTpc/stat .20211112.000001      2021-11-12 00:00:03    iteration 3: iter2 +
               pent       .20211112.000003                              measured deltas.
                                                                        FINAL Run 22
  run23ideal   all three .20221220.000000       2022-12-20 00:00:00    Daniel's identity
                                                                        placeholder
  run24seed    all three .20231210.000000       2023-12-10 00:00:00    Run 24 seeded from the
                                                                        Run 22 finals, copied
                                                                        verbatim -- NOT a
                                                                        Run 24 measurement
  run24iter1   onTpc/stat .20231210.000000      2023-12-10 00:00:01    Run 24 iteration 1:
               pent       .20231210.000001                              first actual Run 24
                                                                        measurement

  Note that pentOnStation was revised on its own several times, so the three
  tables do NOT all have an entry at every timestamp. Each tag above therefore
  names a stamp PER TABLE -- the latest entry at or before that time, which is
  what St_db_Maker itself resolves to. Pushing pentOnStation alone would leave
  the other two at whatever was there before; fttGeometry_db.C always writes
  all three together for that reason.

  The row.comment strings in the newer files are genuinely informative here,
  unlike the FST ones -- e.g. "Station=1(Disk=0),Pent=1(Quad=A,x>0,y>0)
  geo(+0.809,+3.634) + align(+1.331,+1.794)", which separates the geometry term
  from the alignment term. Each file also has a header comment recording how it
  was produced and what it requires.

  One row to know about: station 2, pentagon 1 (quad A) carries a geometry term
  only, with no alignment term -- "NO V HITS" -- so it is not constrained the
  way the other fifteen are.


Open questions on beginTime, settle these BEFORE pushing
--------------------------------------------------------
Gene's rule (database_timestamp.txt) reserves two slots per run year, an
"ideal" date and a "survey/estimate" date, with measured constants from real
data going any time after those. Two of our timestamps do not sit cleanly in
that scheme:

  1. The Run 22 entries use 2021-11-1x. That is in the "real data" range, so it
     is legal, but the reserved Run 22 slots (ideal 2021-10-10, survey
     2021-10-15) were never used for anything but the identity entry. Fine as
     is, but worth stating when we hand these over.

  2. The Run 24 entries use 2023-12-10 and 2023-12-10 00:00:01 -- which IS
     Gene's Run 24 *ideal* slot, holding measured constants. The choice was
     deliberate and is recorded in the file headers: the entry has to precede
     the Run 24 ZF data (event time 2024-06-22) AND beat the 2022-12-20
     identity placeholder, and the ideal slot does both. Strictly, measured
     constants belong after the survey slot. Ask Gene whether he wants these
     moved to 2023-12-15 or later before they go in.

  3. database_timestamp.txt lists the 2024 survey slot as "2024-12-15", which
     cannot be right -- the same year's ideal slot is 2023-12-10 and Run 24 ran
     in 2024, so a 2024-12-15 estimate would postdate the run it describes.
     Almost certainly a typo for 2023-12-15. Confirm with Gene.

The filenames have deliberately NOT been renamed to match the slots, so that
existing local setups and the fGeom caches built against these sdt values keep
working. fttGeometry_db.C sets beginTime explicitly and does not take it from
the filename, so the two can be reconciled without touching the files -- but
decide first, because beginTime is what picks the entry for a given run.



The "misalign" BFC option is what makes any of this take effect
---------------------------------------------------------------
The AGML/XML geometry reads these survey tables ONLY when the bfc "misalign"
option is on. Its entry in BigFullChain.h is

    {"misalign", "", "", "", "", "-AgMLideal", "Misaligned Geometry", kFALSE},

i.e. it works by taking AgMLideal OUT of the chain, which is what lets
AgPosition apply the <Misalign> entries in the geometry XML. Without it the
tables are ignored completely and the geometry is built ideal, however
carefully you edited them -- no warning, no log line, the numbers simply do not
matter.

With "misalign" on, these tables feed BOTH of:

  * fGeom.root generation. build_geom_misalign.C passes misalign=1, so the
    cached geometry is built FROM these tables. The cache is a frozen snapshot:
    once built it no longer consults the tables at all.

  * MC / simulation. The simulated detector is placed with these same
    parameters, so changing a table changes where simulated hits land, not just
    how real hits are reconstructed.

COPYING IS AS DANGEROUS AS LINKING. Both are, for different reasons, and the
copy is the one that actually catches people out:

  * a LINK means someone else's edit, or your own later edit, silently changes
    every job that resolves through it -- reconstruction, geometry building and
    MC alike -- including jobs you meant as a fixed reference.

  * a COPY gives no hint it exists. A symlink at least announces itself in
    "ls -l"; a copied directory looks like an ordinary directory and nothing
    about it says "this is shadowing the release and the database". It also
    goes stale the moment macro/ or the DB is updated, and it keeps winning
    anyway. Weeks later the numbers disagree with everyone else's and the
    reason is a directory you forgot you made.

The real hazard is not copy-versus-link, it is that ./StarDb/Geometry/* EXISTS
AT ALL and silently takes precedence. There is no log line to catch it:
St_db_Maker does not report which of the three sources a table came from, and
StFttDb only says whether it used survey tables, not where they came from
(StFstDb does not even say that much). So check by hand before you trust a
result:

      ls -ld ./StarDb/Geometry/* 2>/dev/null

which prints nothing if you are clean, "d..." if you copied, and the "-> ..."
arrow if you linked. Run it in the job's working directory too, not only where
you submitted from.

Habits that work: keep one working directory per alignment study rather than
editing in place, and delete or rename ./StarDb the moment the study is over,
instead of leaving it for next time.

And be equally careful about WHICH fGeom.root you are using. The cache and the
tables are two separate things that can disagree, and nothing checks that they
match. A stale fGeom built from older tables quietly keeps using the old
alignment while ./StarDb shows the new numbers -- which looks exactly like "the
new alignment had no effect". If you change a table, rebuild the cache, and
name it so the versions are distinguishable: that is what the sdt and label
arguments of build_geom_misalign.C are for.

Pushing to the database
-----------------------
      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("run22iter3")'            dry run
      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("run22iter3","writedb")'  store

Dry run first: it prints every row it would write and touches nothing. Do not
push to Geometry_ftt until #20 and #23 have landed, or production will go on
reading Geometry/stgc and never see these entries.
